Zune68 19.81 - Alpha 1 for AmigaOS 3.x / m68k
==========================================

Zune68 is a MUI-compatible user interface library based on AROS Zune.
The full package contains the core libraries, the Zune preferences editor,
18 MCC/MCP plugins, Rawimage/Pixmap image classes, translation catalogs,
opentest, eight examples,
SDK headers, plugin documentation/licenses, and corresponding source.
The separate core-only packaging option omits the additional components.

Requirements
------------
AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, a 68000 or better, and Amiga Installer 43 or newer.
The system IEEE double math libraries must be available; no FPU is needed.
Installer itself is not included. The Install icon uses the default tool
"installer". If Workbench cannot find it, set the icon's Default Tool to
its full path, such as SYS:System/Installer on AmigaOS 3.2.3.

Installation
------------
Extract the archive and double-click Install inside the Zune68 drawer.
Alternatively, change to that drawer in a Shell and run:

  SYS:System/Installer Install MINUSER AVERAGE

Each library, plugin, and Prefs editor has its own Install/Skip question.
Existing and bundled
versions are shown before anything is replaced. Versions come from the
actual files, not from the archive filename. Unknown versions are shown
as unknown. Equal-version replacements and downgrades require the same
explicit choice as upgrades. The default is Skip.

  zunemaster.library  -> LIBS:zunemaster.library
    Installs alongside MUI. opentest uses this name by default.

  muimaster.library   -> LIBS:muimaster.library
    Replaces MUI for applications using the standard library name.
    Installing this is optional; it can affect other MUI applications.

  Prefs/Zune         -> SYS:Prefs/Zune (with Workbench icon)
    The preferences editor used by application settings menus.

  Libs/MUI/*         -> LIBS:MUI/
    BetterString, HotkeyString, NBitmap, NBalance, NList, NListview,
    NFloattext, NListtree, TextEditor, TheBar, TheBarVirt, TheButton,
    the Toolbar wrapper, and their bundled MCP editors. Rawimage and
    Pixmap supply image rendering for the built-in preferences pages.
    These plugins open the standard muimaster.library name. For use with
    Zune68, install its muimaster.library variant too. Side-by-side tests
    with a different MUI implementation and these plugins are unverified.
    Plugins are loaded on demand, not added to every application's cost.

  Locale/Catalogs/*  -> LOCALE:Catalogs/<system-language>/
    Translation installation is optional. The installer maps package
    language names to Amiga names, for example german to deutsch.
    Incomplete or unconvertible translations fall back to English.

Before replacing a file, the installer saves the original beside it as
<component-name>.zune68-old. An existing backup is preserved. Copy or
rename that backup back to the original name to restore it. A newer
resident library can remain active until reboot; the installer reports
the resident version separately and does not expunge libraries in use.

Catalogs and the Prefs icon have short .old backups. Saved preferences
are not replaced. Tests/opentest, Examples, SDK, Docs, and Source.tar.gz
remain in the extracted package. No SDK assigns are changed, and nothing
changes in S:Startup-Sequence or S:User-Startup.
Pretend mode displays the choices without copying files.

After installation, close applications and reboot before trying Zune68.
Run Tests/opentest from the extracted drawer. To exercise the replacement
name explicitly, run Tests/opentest muimaster.library.

The native executables request at least 65536 bytes of stack and free
any replacement stack on exit.

Normal builds omit Zune drawing/input traces. The test still prints its
own progress and results. Source builds can enable tracing with TRACE=1;
that output is deliberately excluded from performance measurements.

Status and source
-----------------
This is the first alpha release. Compiler and binary-interface checks do not
establish application compatibility or performance on real hardware.
Prefs and the additional plugins still need GUI testing on AmigaOS.
The imported plugins retain compiler warnings; translations are partial.

The preferences editor includes the upstream PrefsWindow implementation.
Other AROS-only external classes and muiscreen.library are not built. Optional Prefs page icons needing Rawimage/Pixmap are
unavailable; the editor has a public-screen enumeration fallback without
muiscreen.library. BetterString uses ordinary text rendering where the
classic CyberGraphX SDK lacks alpha-text support; NBitmap uses its
existing software alpha fallback.

AROS source: https://github.com/aros-development-team/AROS
Source.tar.gz contains the corresponding sources and native build tools.
The supplied LICENSE contains the AROS Public License; component licenses
and copyright notices remain with their sources and under Docs.
